    "available_at": "2026-10-20T21:21:31.882885+00:00",
    "body": "CMB.Tech’s Mineral Europa, described by its Belgian shipowner as the world’s first ammonia-powered dry bulk carrier, has completed sea trials, marking a step toward commercial deployment of the alternative fuel in large ocean-going cargo ships.\nThe 210,000-deadweight-ton Newcastlemax was built at China’s Qingdao Beihai Shipyard and will operate under a charter agreement with Australian miner Fortescue. Its dual-fuel engine can run on ammonia or conventional marine fuel.\nFollowing the trials, the vessel took on 1,000 metric tons of green ammonia in a ship-to-ship bunkering operation at Zhoushan, China. The Mineral Europa departed for Australia on its maiden voyage Oct. 14.\nThe vessel leads a broader fleet expansion. CMB.Tech (NYSE: CMBT ), based in Antwerp, plans deliveries of 21 ammonia-powered ships through 2029, comprising 18 dry bulk carriers, one containership and two chemical tankers. The company said Oct. 9 that most were expected to be delivered within the following nine months.\n“Mineral Europa is the first of its kind, but many more will follow in the months and years ahead,” CMB.Tech Chief Executive Alexander Saverys said in the company’s announcement.\nThe business case for ammonia-powered shipping is decarbonization, not cheaper fuel, as ship owners are betting that long-term cargo contracts, lower emissions-related costs and protection against future regulation, particularly in Europe, will eventually outweigh more expensive ships and fuel. DNV’s assessment is that fuel cost remains the biggest barrier to making that equation work.\nAmmonia-fueled newbuilds typically cost 15%–20% above conventional designs, according to maritime risk analyst DNV, and European green ammonia at about $2,900 per metric ton of marine-gas-oil-equivalent energy is roughly five times that of diesel fuel. Ammonia also requires nearly three times the fuel volume of diesel for equivalent energy, the analyst said, creating trade-offs among tank space, cargo capacity, range and bunkering frequency.\nThe Fortescue charter connects the new propulsion technology with a major dry bulk cargo customer. In June, the mining company agreed to charter up to 12 CMB.Tech Newcastlemax vessels. Up to three are to be delivered with dual-fuel ammonia engines, while the remainder are to be delivered ammonia-ready, according to reports.\nCMB.Tech is also investing in the infrastructure needed to support ammonia-fueled operations, including fuel storage and bunker barges. The company said introducing the ships is only part of the transition, with safe and efficient fuel delivery also needed to support their deployment.\nThe owner is promoting green ammonia as a fuel produced using renewable electricity, water and air, and says ammonia releases no carbon dioxide when burned. That claim concerns combustion of the ammonia itself; Mineral Europa also retains the ability to operate on conventional marine fuel.\nRead more articles by Stuart Chirls here .\nRead more:\nTime-definite ocean delivery? Container ship completes new Arctic route trial\nHow new capacity is raising rates in chaotic ocean market\nNo. 2 container line studying US-UK route for nuclear ships\nTrans-Pacific facing new booking risks as reliability sinks\nContainer ship attacks escalate in Strait of Hormuz\nUpcoming FreightWaves Events\nCompliance\nBrokerage Compliance Symposium\nThe day before F3. Every compliance issue you face - fraud exposure, carrier liability, FMCSA rules, cargo theft, insurance gaps - navigated by attorneys and operators defining best practices in a changing industry.\nOctober 26, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nAwards\nF3 Awards Dinner\nThe night before F3. FreightTech100 companies honored. FreightTech 25 and Shipper of Choice winners revealed live. Cocktail reception into dinner and live music - 300 industry leaders in one purpose-built room.\nOctober 26, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nFreightTech\nF3: Future of Freight Festival\nIndustry-defining keynotes, rapid-fire technology demos, and industry leaders networking in experiences across Chattanooga - plus the inaugural F3 Awards Dinner featuring the FreightTech and Shipper of Choice reveals.\nOctober 27, 2026 – October 28, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nCompliance Brokerage Compliance Symposium Oct 26 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nThe day before F3. Every compliance issue you face - fraud exposure, carrier liability, FMCSA rules, cargo theft, insurance gaps - navigated by attorneys and operators defining best practices in a changing industry.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now\nAwards F3 Awards Dinner Oct 26 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nThe night before F3. FreightTech100 companies honored. FreightTech 25 and Shipper of Choice winners revealed live. Cocktail reception into dinner and live music - 300 industry leaders in one purpose-built room.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now\nFreightTech F3: Future of Freight Festival Oct 27 – Oct 28 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nIndustry-defining keynotes, rapid-fire technology demos, and industry leaders networking in experiences across Chattanooga - plus the inaugural F3 Awards Dinner featuring the FreightTech and Shipper of Choice reveals.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now",
